  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nSurface high pressure continues to be displaced south, now over the \nDelmarva, in response to a deep low east of the Hudson Bay. The \nassociated cold front is bisecting northern Lake Michigan and this \nfront slides southeast through time, stalling out atop the \naformentioned high. The result is increasing high clouds tonight \nthrough Monday. These high clouds may limit radiational cooling \ntonight, reducing the overall fog risk, yet cannot completely \ndiscount at least patchy fog overnight. As northeast flow becomes \nestablished Monday evening, cooler on Tuesday especially north \nof US 30 in the vicinity of the stalled out front. This \nnortheast flow in place early Tuesday morning may bring a Lake \nErie marine layer well inland resulting in areas of dense fog.\n\nWarming back into the mid-70s Wednesday as the cold front moves off \nand high pressure resumes its relentless grip over the eastern \nCONUS (here, nine days and counting rain-free. Guidance \ncontinues to advertise some fractures in this blocking pattern \nwith another front attempting to move to the Midwest. The in- \nhouse blend threatens some 20% POPs over the greater South Bend \narea as early as Thursday, but the meager forcing and abundance \nof preceding dry air leaves me doubtful. Instead, as advertised \nin this space this morning, the next best chance for any \nmeaningful rain is not until Saturday night or Sunday as a \ntrough lifts in from the Southern Plains. The advertised 60% \nPOPs could be a touch high at this distance with guidance \nthreatening a brief high- over- low block over the Midwest, \npassing the aformented trough south. Stay tuned.",
